Suba Asami has never had it easy she  lives in a 10 or 15 square metre  corrugated iron heart with her husband  and son near Kathmandu every now and  then she works as a day laborer at  construction sites or in the fields  earning the equivalent of three or four  euros but she sees no need to complain  and says she's happy the only thing that  really bothers her is discrimination as  a Dalit woman I faced many problems  living here I'm a day laborer when I  work for someone in an upper caste house  they usually don't give me food I have  to go to a cafe at the cafe they ask me  to eat my meal outside and rinse off my  dishes myself Dalits are traditionally  considered impure in the caste system  they're the lowest of the low that often  means being denied access to education  and jobs and above all a lack of respect  [Music]  the centuries-old caste system is rooted  in Hinduism many believe that their own  fate is determined by destiny and with  good karma their soul might be reborn  into a higher caste  [Music]  many Nepalese are deeply religious  especially in rural areas high in the  Himalayan mountains  [Music]  the village of Ravi Opie is a two-hour  drive from Katmandu its slopes are steep  the soil is dry and now the monsoon  season is about to begin  bommana toll on J belongs to the media  caste the mountain Dalits she faces even  more hurdles due to a lack of well-paid  jobs her husband has left the country  now he works in Malaysia's oil industry  but Bheem Allah doesn't know exactly  what he does  last summer he left for at least three  years he's never seen their young  daughter it would be so much better if  my husband could stay here in Nepal if  he'd found a job in farming or some  other kind of job he would be a helping  hand he could take the children to  school or help when someone is sick but  we have no choice thanks to her  husband's earnings Bhima a toll on Jay  has some livestock her house is larger  than some of the others here she used to  mainly tend to the household and work  from time to time as a day laborer  but then her life took an unexpected  turn the international climate  protection and Development Organisation  iCIMOD began providing agricultural  training to women in the region giving  priority to those whose husbands have  left to find work a project coordinator  from a local NGO pays regular visits  today he's showing the women the correct  way to plant tomatoes we practically  teach how to grow the plants or how they  can change the you know chemical  pesticides to the by pesticides so it's  not about teaching them it's about demo  organising demonstration plots practical  training authorizing field schools so  this is how we are training Lisa once I  wasn't all that interested in  agriculture and I only use traditional  farming methods  which don't provide much income after  the first people left to find work the  ones left behind just sat idle but now  we understand agriculture and we can put  our time to good use for many local  Dalits the program has been a boon the  district administration believes that  giving opportunities to the lowest caste  is long overdue the caste system in the  PHA follows a centuries-old philosophy  it divides society into forecasts with  Dalits at the bottom dollars have long  been part of our labor force supporting  development and improving society in our  societies undergoing very rapid change  it's becoming more inclusive this old  caste based way of thinking will be  eradicated very soon Dalits aren't so  optimistic they fear the long standing  discrimination they face won't change  anytime soon yet many Nepalese  especially the younger generation dream  of a country without a caste system the  village of Culture BC where EC mode has  launched its training program shows what  this future might look like the local  women many of them Dalits have joined  forces their farming operation is  thriving they'd invented a new kind of  organic fertilizer made 